# Patch files are applied by `git apply` inside the fetched dependency trees,
# where the checked-out sources are LF (git inside the emscripten container has
# no autocrlf). On a Windows host with core.autocrlf=true a plain checkout
# rewrites them to CRLF, and the patch then fails to apply against LF sources -
# a fresh-clone failure with no local symptom, because a working tree that is
# never re-checked out keeps whatever bytes it was written with.
# `-text` disables EOL conversion for these files on every platform.
patches/*.patch -text

# Both pin tables are generated (profiles/boards.json -> build-tools/generate-pinnames.js)
# and `node build-tools/generate-pinnames.js --check` fails when the checked-in
# file differs from what the generator produces. The generator emits LF; with
# core.autocrlf=true (this machine, and any Windows contributor) a checkout
# would rewrite the file to CRLF and the drift check would then report a
# difference that is not one. `-text` keeps the bytes as generated, on every
# platform, which is what makes that check meaningful.
mbed-simulator-hal/targets/TARGET_SIMULATOR/PinNames.h -text
viewer/js-hal/pinnames.js -text

# test/run-t4-tls.js byte-compares the CA embedded in
# test/fixtures/test-tls/test_ca_cert.h against certs/ca.crt. With
# core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout rewrites that PEM to CRLF, the
# comparison fails, and the harness reports a certificate mismatch that has
# nothing to do with the certificate. `-text` keeps the PEM as committed.
test/fixtures/test-tls/certs/*.crt -text
test/fixtures/test-tls/certs/*.key -text
